   I just finished another game with the patch (a monk, killed by a death
ray from an Aleax of Chih Sung-tzu), this time with no really interesting
pets. Humanoids really beat everything else hands-down, I guess; my high
hopes for my mumak and my swarm of killer bees were quickly dashed.

   The only thing I noticed different about this game was not due to your
patch, I'm pretty sure --- I saw a lot more of the "indecisive monster"
bug, in which an attacking monster switches between melee and ranged 
weapon repeatedly, instead of moving or fighting. In one case, I was in
the middle of an open room facing a line of elves, like this:

       @ @@@ @@
       ^ ^^^^^^
      me Green-elves

and the head Green-elf just kept switching weapons. The others didn't
do anything visible at all; I assumed they thought they were stuck
behind the head elf. Eventually I walked up to the head elf --- still
switching weapons --- and killed him, in about three turns during which
he /still/ didn't fight back.

   Scanning through your patch to make sure it couldn't have any
deleterious effect on hostile monsters' behavior, I noticed the following
problem:

+	//You_feel("that monsters are aware of %s presence.",
+	//    s_suffix(mon_nam(mtmp)));

   C99-style comments aren't portable to all NetHack systems. Use #if 0
to "comment out" a block of code, or just turn it into an English
comment explaining what should happen but doesn't, or whatever the
commenting-out is supposed to indicate.
   (There are more C99-style comments elsewhere in the patch.)

+	        pline("%s %s %s!",
+		      s_suffix(Monnam(mtmp)),
+		      xname(otmp),
[...]
+		      is_gloves(otmp) ? "vanish" :
+		      is_boots(otmp)  ? "disintegrate" :

   I haven't tested this, but I think this would print "The gnome's
pair of gloves vanish!", which isn't quite right. Nethack is careful
to insert "pair of" where necessary to keep everything singular, to
make grammar less of a nightmare.
   If xname(otmp) really does return "gloves", then I stand corrected.

+	        You("transforms a clump of sticks into snakes!");

+		    pline("%s reaches out with its deadly touch.",
+		          Monnam(magr));

   Should be a gender-specific pronoun instead of "its".

   mfind_target() looks much more complicated than it needs to be. I
think I see what it's doing --- "find a line on which all the monsters
are enemies of myself, or otherwise expendable, and return the last
monster in that line" --- but for example, it could start out with

     for (origdir = dir = rn2(8); ...

instead of

     origdir = -1;
     for (dir = rn2(8); ...
         if (origdir < 0)
             origdir = dir;

and some of the test conditions should be swapped around, too. My
first impression was that you could do away with all the
'mret = (struct monst *)0' lines, too, but now I'm not sure. Still,
there's room for improvement.

+		    /* breath runs out sometimes. Also, give monster some
+		     * cunning; don't breath if the player fell asleep.
+		     */

   The verb is "breathe". :)

   Well, that was a little more in-depth than my last critique, but
even so, I realize that I'm mostly nitpicking superficial details,
not trying to understand the behavior of the patch as a whole.
Sorry. ;)

